/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Fuel flow is a mass flow in kg/h, because combustion needs a fixed air mass per fuel mass.
   - Volumetric method: the engine draws fuel from a glass burette with two marks setting volume V.
   - ṁf is fuel mass flow in kg/h; V is burette volume in L; ρ is fuel density in kg/L at fuel temperature (about 0.83 diesel, 0.74 petrol); t is time in s; 3600 converts s to h.
   - The three-way cock lets the engine run from the tank while the burette refills, so filling time is never timed.
   - Small volume and short time increase error.
   - Gravimetric method weighs fuel from a can on a balance and clocks the time to lose a set mass, say 100 g.
   - Air is harder to meter because intake flow pulses every stroke, so a settling volume smooths it.
   - Air box: a large tank the engine breathes from, with a sharp-edged orifice open to the room.
   - ṁa is air mass flow in kg/s; Cd is discharge coefficient (about 0.6 sharp-edged orifice, 0.98 venturi); A is orifice area in m²; ρ is air density in kg/m³; Δp is pressure drop in Pa, equal to 9.81 × h for h in mm of water.
   - Air density comes from the test room: ρ = p ÷ (R T), where p is absolute room pressure in Pa, T is room temperature in K, and R = 287 J/(kg·K) for air.
*/
