/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A dynamometer must do two things: take the engine's power, and measure the torque.
   - In the Prony brake a lined block is clamped on the flywheel and a lever carries the reaction to a spring balance.
   - BP is brake power in W; N is engine speed in rev/min; T is torque in N·m.
   - An absorption brake can only load the engine.
   - A transient dynamometer goes further.
   - An absorption dynamometer cannot do this.
   - A test cell is a room designed around the dynamometer.
   - Services are fixed: fuel supply with a measured tank or flow meter (see the chapter on fuel and air measurement), coolant circuit with a heat exchanger and temperature control, ventilation to supply combustion air and remove fumes, exhaust 
   - The instrument panel logs speed, torque, temperatures and flows at the same time.
*/
